  "query_preview": "..\n\nSurface warm front still in the process of lifting across the \ncentral portions of lower MI eventually clears to the north by late \nmorning ushering in respectable southerly warm advection. 850mb temps\nquickly rise into the mid teens C supporting high temps in the mid \nto upper 70s. \n\nRemnants of Isaias are expected to track along the western spine of \nthe southern Appalachians tonight into Sunday before deflecting into \nthe mid-Atlantic by Sunday night. Locally, the consequence of this \nmore southerly track is the bulk of the northern rain shield holds \nto our south and east over Ohio/Lake Erie. As such, there has been a \nnotable downward trend in most model QPF towards a more 0.1-0.25\" \nrange along the St. Clair-Erie corridor and Ohio border areas- where \nhighest confidence in showers exist. Shower coverage, duration, and \nQPF decreases further north and west you go outside of Metro Detroit \nas building western Great Lakes mid-level ridging noses in towards \nthe Saginaw Bay Sunday. Rain chances come to an end by Sunday \nevening as the system shifts east of the Appalachians. \n\nDry, very mild conditions remain in place for Monday as mid-level \nridging flattens overtop of lower MI. This collapsing ridge allows \nthe core of the upper Midwest airmass to fold into the area bringing \n850mb temps towards the upper teens C leading to widespread 70s with \na shot at some seeing 80. A shift back to a more typical fall \npattern arrives by Monday night-Tuesday as a cold front sagging out \nof northern Ontario finally reaches/crosses the central Great Lakes. \nUpper Plains mid-level shortwave rides along this frontal boundary \nas it crosses supporting the region's next chances for scattered \nshowers. Post-frontal airmass marks the return of average \ntemperatures with mid/late week highs in the lower 60s and overnight \nlows in the lower 40s.",
